      "dom_id": "question-46152",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The speed of a car moving with a constant acceleration is given by the formula $v=at,$ where $a$ is the car's acceleration, and $t$ is the time passed. Given that the acceleration is measured in $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}$, determine the units of speed in terms of meters and seconds.",
      "worked_solution": "Acceleration is measured in $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}$ and time is measured in seconds. We can find the unit of speed by substituting the given units of acceleration and time into the formula and simplifying. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =a\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}\\,\\cancel{\\text{s}}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\cancel{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of speed is $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.$",
